Q: Swing navigation - basic A: <p>When you start up the application, you should only ever create one JFrame. Then have a private JPanel for putting content. Create a ReplacablePanel class that stores an invoker and has a back button. Other screens should extend ReplacablePanel. Now when you click the button to go to another screen, switch to another ReplacablePanel and make it aware of the invoker.</p>
<pre><code>class ReplacablePanel extends JPanel {
    private JPanel invoker;
    private JButton back;

    public void replacePanel(JPanel newPanel) {
        SwingUtilities.invokeLater(() -&gt; {
            Container parent = getParent();
            if (parent != null) {
                LayoutManager layout = parent.getLayout();
                if (layout instanceof GridBagLayout) {
                    GridBagLayout gbl = (GridBagLayout) layout;
                    GridBagConstraints gbc = gbl.getConstraints(this);
                    parent.remove(this);
                    parent.add(newPanel, gbc);
                } else {
                    parent.remove(this);
                    parent.add(newPanel);
                }
                parent.revalidate();
                parent.repaint();
            }
        });
    }

    public ReplacablePanel() {
        // no back button for root panels
    }

    public ReplacablePanel(JPanel invoker) {
        this.invoker = invoker;

        setLayout(new GridBagLayout());
        back = new JButton(&quot;Back&quot;);
        back.addActionListener(e -&gt; replacePanel(this.invoker));

        // bottom right corner
        GridBagConstraints gbc = new GridBagConstraints();
        gbc.anchor = GridBagConstraints.LAST_LINE_END;
        add(back, gbc);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Here is an example of using it (put this in the main application class):</p>
<pre><code>    private void switchToPanel(ReplacablePanel panel) {
        mainPanel.removeAll();
        mainPanel.add(panel);
        mainPanel.revalidate();
        mainPanel.repaint();
    }
</code></pre>
